#|
EXERCISES
1.  Write a function that returns the intersection of two arrays. The intersec
tion is a third array that contains all values contained within the first two
arrays. For example, the intersection of [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] and [0, 2, 4, 6, 8] is [2, 4].
Your function should have a complexity of O(N). (If your programming
language has a built-in way of doing this, don’t use it. The idea is to build
the algorithm yourself.)


answer:
|#

; functiona/list approach
; not sure how i can achieve O(n), maybe with hash table?

; REGULAR
; probably not O(n) since it uses membership check on each xs
(define (intersection xs ys)
  ; fix xs, check each item in xs in ys
  (cond [(null? xs) '()]
        [(member (car xs) ys) (cons (car xs) (intersection (cdr xs) ys)) ] ;put it
        [else (intersection (cdr xs) ys)] ;skip it
        )
  )
(intersection '(1 2 3 4 5) '(0 2 4 6 8)) ; '(2 4) 

; WITH HASH TABLE
; should be O(n)
(define (intersection-hash xs ys)
  (let ((ht (make-hash)))
    ; fill hash with xs, just store as key
    ; iterate ys and check if it exists, if so add to result
    (define (fill-ht lst)
      (cond [(null? lst) 'done]
            [else (begin (hash-set! ht (car lst) #t)
                         (fill-ht (cdr lst))
                         ) ]
            )
      )

    (define (loop lst)
      (cond [(null? lst) '()]
            [else (let ((key (hash-has-key? ht (car lst))))
                    (if key
                        (cons (car lst) (loop (cdr lst)))
                        (loop (cdr lst))
                        )
                    )
                  ]
            )
      )

    ; ht now stores xs
    (fill-ht xs)

    ; iterate through ys
    (loop ys)
    
    )
  )
(intersection-hash '(1 2 3 4 5) '(0 2 4 6 8))

